     "type": "text",
     "text": "{\"count\": 1, \"rationale\": \"With no defences, exfiltration is possible. The attacker phishes a workstation (always available, giving foothold_workstation and stolen_user), pivots internal, authenticates to the appserver, kerberoasts to get stolen_admin, authenticates to the DC and escalates, dumps stolen_dbadmin, then authenticates to the DB, escalates and exfiltrates. So the answer is at least 1. Every exfiltration technique needs admin on db, fileserver or backup. Those need a foothold from auth_db, auth_fileserver or auth_backup, which all require stolen_dbadmin. The only sources of stolen_dbadmin are dump_dbadmin_dc and dump_dbadmin_adfs, which need admin_dc or admin_adfs. Those need foothold_dc or foothold_adfs, and the only techniques giving them (auth_dc, auth_adfs) require NOT mfa_admin. Deploying mfa:admin alone therefore blocks stolen_dbadmin and so all exfiltration. The minimum is 1.\"}"
    }
   ],
